2. Solve the First Problem: Comparative DNA Analysis

2.1 Represent DNA mathematically

At each genetic position, an individual may carry different alleles.

For a simplified analysis using two alleles at a genetic position, encode the genotype as:

g_{ij}\in\{0,1,2\}

Where:

* g_{ij}=0: two copies of the reference allele.
* g_{ij}=1: one copy of each allele.
* g_{ij}=2: two copies of the alternative allele.

Here, i identifies the individual and j identifies the genetic position.

The genotype matrix is:

\mathbf G=
\begin{bmatrix}
g_{11}&g_{12}&\cdots&g_{1m}\\
g_{21}&g_{22}&\cdots&g_{2m}\\
\vdots&\vdots&\ddots&\vdots\\
g_{n1}&g_{n2}&\cdots&g_{nm}
\end{bmatrix}

Here, n is the number of individuals and m is the number of genetic positions examined.

The first task is to compare an individual’s genetic variation with variation observed in reference populations.

However, not all genetic positions are equally informative. Closely linked positions are correlated, some variants are rare, and technical errors can distort comparisons.

The model must account for these factors rather than treating every genetic position as independent evidence.

2.2 Calculate genetic distance

A simple starting point is a standardized genetic distance between two individuals:

D(i,k)=
\frac{1}{m}
\sum_{j=1}^{m}
w_j\left|g_{ij}-g_{kj}\right|

Where:

* D(i,k) measures genetic dissimilarity.
* w_j is a weight assigned to genetic position j.
* g_{ij} and g_{kj} are the genotypes of individuals i and k.

This equation is useful for illustrating genetic comparison, but it is not sufficient to establish ancestry.

Why not?

Two populations may share genetic variants because of common ancestry, historical migration, or gene flow. An individual may also be genetically similar to several populations that share a complex demographic history.

Furthermore, a raw distance score does not distinguish recent genealogical relationships from older population-level relationships.

Consequently, the Mathematical Future Ancestry Object needs a more powerful comparison method.

2.3 Compare allele frequencies between populations

Let:

p_{kj}=P(\text{alternative allele at position }j
\mid \text{population }k)

This is the frequency of an allele in population k at genetic position j.

The model compares the individual’s observed genotypes against the allele-frequency distributions of candidate populations.

For example, suppose a genetic position has the following hypothetical allele frequencies:

Population	Alternative allele frequency
Population A	0.80
Population B	0.45
Population C	0.10

An individual carrying the alternative allele may have a genotype more probable under Population A’s allele-frequency distribution than under Population C’s.

But one genetic position cannot establish that the individual descends from Population A.

A meaningful inference requires many genetic positions, appropriate reference samples, and a model that accounts for population structure and relatedness.

