9. The Ethical Constraint: Ancestry Is Not Identity

The Mathematical Future Ancestry Object should include an explicit constraint:

\boxed{
\text{Genetic ancestry}
\neq
\text{ethnic identity}
\neq
\text{nationality}
\neq
\text{cultural belonging}
}

These categories can overlap, but they are not interchangeable.

Genetic evidence can help investigate population history. It cannot independently determine a person’s cultural identity, citizenship, moral worth, or rightful membership in a community.

Similarly, the model should not use genetic similarity as a justification for racial hierarchy or discrimination.

For population-level research, it should also distinguish genetic population structure from socially defined racial categories. The latter do not correspond neatly to discrete biological divisions.

Finally, individual DNA data are sensitive information. A practical implementation should establish informed consent, access controls, secure storage, appropriate data-sharing policies, and restrictions on secondary uses.

These constraints should be part of the mathematical system’s design rather than added after the model has been built.

