10. What Would It Take to Build This for Real?

The mathematical framework is feasible, but a working ancestry-discovery system would require data, software, validation, and carefully chosen research objectives.

Existing methods already address important parts of this problem.

Research objective	Established methodological approach
Estimate ancestry proportions	Model-based ancestry inference and genetic mixture models
Identify genetic population structure	Principal component analysis and population clustering
Compare allele-frequency differences	Population-genetic statistics, including F_{ST}
Estimate relationships between populations	Coalescent and demographic models
Detect admixture	Admixture statistics and ancestry-aware haplotype methods
Investigate ancient population movements	Ancient DNA analysis and demographic inference
Quantify uncertainty	Bayesian inference, bootstrap methods, and sensitivity analysis
Detect inadequate reference coverage	Predictive validation and model comparison

These methods are complementary. No single method answers every question about ancestry.

A practical implementation could combine them into a reproducible pipeline, with each component independently validated.

It should also benchmark its performance against established methods before claiming that the Mathematical Future Ancestry Object improves ancestry inference.

A proposed research architecture

The first prototype should focus on one well-defined task:

Given genetic data and a set of reference populations, estimate ancestral population contributions, identify when the available references are insufficient, and quantify uncertainty in the resulting estimates.

A second stage could add ancient DNA and historical demographic reconstruction.

A third stage could investigate whether the combined framework improves predictive accuracy, calibration, or robustness over established approaches.

This staged approach is more scientifically defensible than attempting to reconstruct every ancestral population and migration event simultaneously.

